        </p>
        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[#163824] font-semibold tracking-tight mt-10 mb-4 pt-6 border-t border-[#12281b]/10">
          <span>Malayan Sun Bear</span>
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          The Malayan Sun Bear is the smallest bear species in the world and one of the most elusive mammals found in Khao Sok National Park. It is easily recognised by the distinctive orange or yellow crescent-shaped patch on its chest. Sun Bears spend much of their time foraging in the rainforest for fruit, insects, honey and other food.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Despite its relatively small size, the Sun Bear is a powerful animal with strong claws and jaws. Its unusually long tongue helps it extract insects and honey from nests hidden within trees and fallen logs.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Sightings of Malayan Sun Bears are rare in Khao Sok and should be considered a fortunate wildlife encounter. However, they are occasionally spotted during overnight lake trips and jungle camping adventures. Remote areas of the rainforest offer the best chance of a sighting.
        </p>
