        </p>
        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[#163824] font-semibold tracking-tight mt-10 mb-4 pt-6 border-t border-[#12281b]/10">
          <span>Praying Mantis</span>
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          The Praying Mantis is one of the rainforest's most effective ambush predators. With its slender body, triangular head and folded forelegs, it is instantly recognisable once spotted, although its remarkable camouflage often allows it to remain hidden in plain sight.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Mantises rely on patience rather than speed. Remaining perfectly still, they wait for insects and other small prey to come within reach before striking with lightning-fast reflexes. Larger species have even been known to capture small reptiles, frogs and birds.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          One of the most fascinating features of the Praying Mantis is its ability to rotate its head and carefully track movement around it. Combined with its excellent camouflage, this makes it one of the most successful hunters in the rainforest.
        </p>
        <figure class="my-10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl border border-[#12281b]/10 bg-white/50">
