        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[#163824] font-semibold tracking-tight mt-10 mb-4 pt-6 border-t border-[#12281b]/10">
          <span>RAINFOREST PLANTS</span>
        </h2>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          The ancient rainforest of Khao Sok National Park supports an extraordinary variety of plant life.<br /> High rainfall, limestone karst formations and a warm tropical climate have created ideal conditions for an exceptionally rich and diverse ecosystem.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          The rainforest supports everything from towering trees and giant bamboo thickets to unusual climbing plants and rare flowering species.<br /> Among the most remarkable plants found in Khao Sok are the giant Rafflesia flower, carnivorous pitcher plants, massive dipterocarp trees and strangling figs.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Together, these plants help create the lush rainforest landscape that makes Khao Sok one of Thailand's most distinctive natural destinations.
        </p>
        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[#163824] font-semibold tracking-tight mt-10 mb-4 pt-6 border-t border-[#12281b]/10">
          <span>Rafflesia</span>
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          The Rafflesia is one of the most extraordinary plants found in Khao Sok National Park and is famous for producing the largest flower in the world. The species found in Khao Sok, Rafflesia kerrii, can reach impressive sizes and is one of the rainforest's most sought-after natural attractions.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          With its bright red petals covered in white spots, the flower is unmistakable when in bloom. It also produces a strong odour similar to rotting meat, which attracts flies that help pollinate the flower. This unusual characteristic has earned Rafflesia the nickname "Corpse Flower".
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Unlike most plants, Rafflesia is a parasite that grows on the roots and stems of wild grape vines. The flower blooms for only a short period, making sightings particularly special. The best time to see Rafflesia in Khao Sok is generally between December and March, although flowering depends on local conditions and cannot be guaranteed.
        </p>
