        </figure>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          <strong>Best Viewing Opportunity:</strong> <a href="/khao-sok-trekking-hiking/#flower" class="text-[#1b432c] underline decoration-[#ba9038]/60 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-[#ba9038] hover:text-[#7a5813] transition-colors">Rafflesia Flower Trek</a>
        </p>
        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[#163824] font-semibold tracking-tight mt-10 mb-4 pt-6 border-t border-[#12281b]/10">
          <span>Pitcher Plant</span>
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          The Tropical Pitcher Plant is one of the most fascinating plants found in the Khao Sok rainforest. Unlike most plants, it is carnivorous, trapping and digesting insects to obtain nutrients from the nutrient-poor soils in which it grows.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Its distinctive pitcher-shaped leaves contain a liquid that attracts, traps and eventually digests insects. Bright colours, nectar and scent lure prey to the rim of the pitcher, where the slippery surface causes them to fall inside.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Pitcher plants are commonly found in the forests of Southern Thailand and are particularly associated with humid rainforest environments. Their unusual appearance and remarkable hunting strategy make them a favourite among nature enthusiasts exploring Khao Sok.
        </p>
        <figure class="my-10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl border border-[#12281b]/10 bg-white/50">
          <img
            src="/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/pitcher-plant-2.jpg"
            alt="Pitcher plant - Khao national park"
            class="w-full h-52 sm:h-72 md:h-80 object-cover object-center"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
           width="1200" height="800" />
          <figcaption class="p-3.5 text-xs text-[#255038] font-serif italic border-t border-[#12281b]/10 bg-[#f4efeb]/60">Pitcher plant - Khao national park</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[#163824] font-semibold tracking-tight mt-10 mb-4 pt-6 border-t border-[#12281b]/10">
          <span>Dipterocarp Tree</span>
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Dipterocarp trees are among the most important trees in the Khao Sok rainforest. Many species produce distinctive winged seeds that spin through the air like tiny helicopters when they fall from the canopy.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          The name Dipterocarp means "two-winged fruit", referring to these remarkable seed pods. By spinning and gliding away from the parent tree, the seeds can travel surprising distances and establish new trees elsewhere in the forest.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Many Dipterocarp species grow into towering rainforest giants, forming part of the upper canopy and providing habitat for birds, insects and other wildlife. Their winged seeds are one of the most fascinating examples of how plants have adapted to life in the rainforest.
        </p>
