        </figure>
        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[#163824] font-semibold tracking-tight mt-10 mb-4 pt-6 border-t border-[#12281b]/10">
          <span>Fig Tree</span>
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Fig trees are among the most important plants in the Khao Sok rainforest. Their fruits provide a vital food source for many animals, including hornbills, gibbons, macaques and other wildlife throughout the year.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          One of the most remarkable aspects of the fig tree is its relationship with tiny fig wasps. These insects pollinate the flowers hidden inside the developing fruit, creating a unique partnership that has evolved over millions of years. Without fig wasps, there would be no figs, and without figs, the wasps could not survive.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Another fascinating member of the fig family is the Strangler Fig. Although it eventually resembles a tree, it begins life as a seed deposited high in the branches of another tree by birds or other animals. As it grows, roots descend towards the ground and gradually surround the host tree. Over many years, the host may die and decay, leaving behind the hollow centre often associated with mature Strangler Figs.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Figs play a vital role in maintaining the rich biodiversity of the Khao Sok rainforest and are considered one of the ecosystem's most important plant groups.
        </p>
        <figure class="my-10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl border border-[#12281b]/10 bg-white/50">
